{"ts": 1789778232.945146, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Sign checks confirm {24+, 36\u2212, 17+} is the right orientation \u2014 flipping any sign drops the Sharpe sharply (1.130 \u2192 0.24/0.39/0.50). The signal is real and directional, not an artifact.\n\nI'll select **features {24, 36, 17} with signs {+1, \u22121, +1}**, in-sample Sharpe **1.130**.\n\nNow calibrating my OOS forecast honestly. Sources of expected decay:\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I ran ~90 evaluations and picked the maximum. The three components were chosen as the top performers from 40, and the triple as the best of many. Even with genuine signal, the winner's in-sample estimate is biased upward.\n\n2. **Magnitude of the bias.** Standard error on annualized Sharpe over 5000 daily obs is \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22. The selection was effectively over ~40 standalone choices plus combination search. A reasonable winner's-curse adjustment is on the order of 0.2\u20130.3 Sharpe.\n\n3. **Mitigating evidence.** Two facts argue the signal is substantially real rather than pure overfit: the diversification gain (0.77 \u2192 0.99 \u2192 1.13) follows the pattern of genuinely uncorrelated signals combining, not noise stacking; and the sweep of all 40 weak features as a third leg produced *nothing* competitive, which is what you'd see if signal is concentrated in a few true features rather than scattered noise that a greedy search could exploit anywhere.\n\nSo I expect decay but not collapse. Centering on roughly **0.85**, with an SD of **0.25** to cover estimation error, regime shift, and residual overfitting \u2014 leaving real probability on both a ~0.4 disappointment and a ~1.2 confirmation."}
