{"ts": 1789778284.331132, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** features {24, 36, 17} with signs {+1, \u22121, +1} \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.130**. Predicted OOS Sharpe: **0.85 \u00b1 0.25**.\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Full univariate sweep** of all 40 features. Standouts: 24 (+0.77), 36 (\u22120.65), 17 (+0.54), 5 (+0.51), 10 (\u22120.45).\n2. **Pairwise tests** among leaders \u2014 {24+, 36\u2212} reached 0.990, well above either leg alone, indicating low correlation.\n3. **Triples**, giving {24+, 36\u2212, 17+} at 1.130.\n4. **Exhaustive third-leg scan**: tested all 37 remaining features against the {24+, 36\u2212} base.\n5. **Sign verification** on the winner.\n\n### Two checks that shaped my confidence\n\nThe exhaustive third-leg scan was the most informative test I ran. If the apparent signal were mostly noise that a greedy search could mine anywhere, some weak feature would have surfaced as a strong complement. None did \u2014 every one landed below the two-feature base. Signal is genuinely concentrated in the features that were strong standalone.\n\nSign-flipping also degraded performance sharply (1.130 \u2192 0.24/0.39/0.50), consistent with real directional signal rather than a fragile fit.\n\n### On the forecast\n\nI shrank the prediction from 1.130 to 0.85 rather than reporting the in-sample number. I selected this spec as the maximum over ~90 evaluations, so the winner's estimate is upward-biased by construction. With SE \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22 on annualized Sharpe, a winner's-curse adjustment of ~0.25 is appropriate. Worth flagging: only feature 24 clearly cleared the selection-noise threshold standalone (the max of 40 null draws is ~0.55), so some of the contribution from 36 and 17 may be overstated \u2014 the diversification pattern and sign tests are what persuaded me they carry real signal. The 0.25 SD keeps meaningful weight on outcomes near 0.4 as well as near 1.2.\n\nOne caveat on scope: all evaluation was in-sample on a single 5000-day panel, so I could not test stability across sub-periods or regimes. If that data becomes available, splitting the sample chronologically would be the highest-value next check."}
